            "snippets": [
              "# Beijing furious as Huawei charges revealed on eve of trade talks\n**Beijing:** China has accused the United States of using state power to \"stifle the just and legitimate operations\" of telecommunications giant Huawei afer FBI director Christopher Wray fronted the media to unveil the indictment against Huawei and its executive Meng Wanzhou.\n\n\"We strongly urge the US to stop the unreasonable suppression of Chinese companies including Huawei,\" the Chinese Foreign Ministry said in a statement.",
              "Meng is accused of bank fraud and wire fraud, while Huawei and a company alleged to be its subsidiary are accused of violating US sanctions on Iran.\n\nThe indictment was made public by Wray and Commerce Secretary Wilbur Ross as Chinese Vice Premier Liu He arrived in Washington for tense trade talks. He was accompanied by a heavy-weight delegation including the governor of the People's Bank of China, Yi Gang.",
              "In Beijing, reaction was swift. The director of the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, Wen Ku, said it was \"neither fair not ethical to use state power to discredit and attack specific companies without any evidence\".",
              "The Chinese Foreign Ministry \"urged the US to immediately revoke the arrest warrant against Meng Wanzhou and not to issue a formal extradition request to avoid going further and further along the wrong path\".\n\nAdvertisement\n\nBut Canadian justice department officials confirmed the extradition request was lodged by the US on Monday",
              "Canadian media reported this was the first step in a process that would require Canadian Justice Department lawyers to decide within 30 days whether to issue an \"authority to proceed\".\n\nA judge would then decide whether there was sufficient evidence to \"commit the person for trial in Canada if the conduct had occurred in this country\".\n\nCanada's justice minister must ultimately decide whether to extradite Meng to the US.",
              "Canada's ambassador to China John McCallum was sacked last week after publicly commenting that Meng had a strong case to fight extradition because Canada doesn't recognise US sanctions on Iran.\n\nOn Tuesday, Beijing continued its campaign of pressure on Canada, urging the Trudeau Government to release Meng and not act as \"the cat's paw\" of the United States.\n\nThe US accuses Huawei of selling products in Iran through a subsidiary, Skycom, and alleges Meng misled international banks about the relationship",
              "The file was \"suggested talking points\" to banks about Huawei's operation in Iran  - it said Skycom was an agent for Huawei and was required to abide by export controls.\n\nChina's Huawei has been barred from 5G networks in several countries.Bloomberg\n\nAnother indictment against Huawei lodged separately in Washington accuses the company of stealing trade secrets, and includes details of internal Huawei emails such as Chinese head office directing staff at its American subsidiary to collect information about a phone-testing robot that had been developed by a Huawei customer, T-Mobile",
              "More internal emails to Huawei USA staff in 2013 describe a \"bonus program\" where staff would be rewarded for \"collecting confidential information regarding our competitors and sending it back\".\n\nAdvertisement\n\nAddressing the media in New York, Wray said: \"Companies like Huawei pose a dual threat to both our economic and national security.\"\n\nRoss, who stood with him, said the legal action against Huawei was unrelated to trade negotiations with China",
              "Huawei said in a statement it was disappointed to learn of the charges, and claimed that after Meng's arrest, it had sought to discuss the investigation with the US Department of Justice but the request was rejected.\n\nThe company denied the allegations and said it was \"not aware of any wrong doing by Ms Meng and believes the US courts will ultimately reach the same conclusion\".",
              "It said the allegations of trade secret theft in the Washington indictment had already been settled in a civil case with T-Mobile, which found neither damages nor wilful and malicious conduct."
